---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';

const title = 'How one SVG becomes a favicon set - favseed';
const description = 'The steps from one SVG to favicon.ico, an apple-touch-icon and maskable PWA icons: SVG optimisation, safe-zone placement, the accuracy bar and Zopfli.';
const lede = 'One SVG goes in and five files come out, six with a manifest. The same code runs every step on the command line, in the Vite and Astro integrations, and on this site.';

const efforts = [
  ['fast', 'One encode at quality 80 with the full 256-colour palette. Nothing is scored.', 'none'],
  ['default', 'A binary search of each list: 7 to 9 encodes, the smallest that passes.', '15 iterations'],
  ['max', 'All 26 candidates, the smallest that passes.', '120 iterations'],
];
---
<Doc {title} {description} heading="How it works" {lede}>
  <section>
    <h2>1. The SVG stage</h2>
    <p>favseed checks that the file holds an <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> element, then optimises it with SVGO. CSS custom
      properties are resolved first: <code>var(--brand, #0E7C68)</code> becomes its fallback unless you
      set <code>--var brand=…</code>. The renderer has no <code>var()</code> support and would draw the whole mark
      black, so a <code>var()</code> with neither a fallback nor a value is an error rather than a black square.</p>
    <p>Animation, styling and accessibility survive by default: CSS and SMIL animation, the classes and ids something
      references, <code>role</code>, and the ids that ARIA attributes name.
      A <code>@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)</code> block stays too, so a favicon drawn for dark tab strips keeps
      working. The result is one <code>icon.svg</code> for the favicon link and for images on your
      pages. <code>--no-animation</code> writes a static SVG instead.</p>
    <p>Coordinates are rounded to as few decimals as the mark allows. favseed tries 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 8 decimals in
      turn and keeps the first whose static render at 512 px has at most 1% of pixels off by more than 8/255, on a
      black and on a white background. If none passes, <code>icon.svg</code> keeps the source's own numbers and the
      build warns. A wide or tall viewBox keeps its proportions: the rasters centre it on a square canvas,
      and <code>icon.svg</code> keeps the original viewport.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Placement in the safe zone</h2>
    <p>Every platform that installs a web app shows the icon through a shape of its own: a circle, a squircle, a
      rounded square. The W3C Web App Manifest names the area every one of those masks shows, a circle centred on the
      icon with a radius of 40% of its size. iOS's rounded square contains that circle whole, so the apple-touch-icon
      and the manifest icons are all placed the same way.</p>
    <p>With <code>--padding auto</code>, the default, favseed renders the mark at each icon's own size, finds the
      opaque pixel farthest from the centre, and scales the mark until that pixel's outer corner sits on the safe
      circle. A mark drawn with its own margin is enlarged and one that reaches its corners is shrunk. No fixed
      percentage suits every shape: across the test fixtures <code>auto</code> chooses between 7.6% and 18.8% per
      side. A number, such as <code>--padding 12</code>, is applied as given.</p>
    <p>An exact fit scales by an arbitrary factor, which puts the edges of a mark drawn on a grid between pixels. So
      favseed then walks the size down in whole pixels, by up to 10%, and takes the size with the fewest partly
      covered pixels, if that at least halves them. On a seven-block grid mark at 512 px the box goes from 330 to
      320 px, the colours from 12 to 3, and the file from 571 to 290 bytes. A curved mark, anti-aliased at every
      size, keeps its exact fit. A source that is opaque in all four corners is full-bleed and is used as drawn.</p>
    <p>The padded icons sit on the <code>--bg</code> ground, <code>#000000</code> unless you choose another. That is
      what iOS composites a transparent icon onto anyway, and a maskable icon has to be opaque, so a colour with
      transparency is refused. <code>--bg none</code> keeps the whole set transparent and unpadded,
      declared <code>any</code> only. The ICO is never padded.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Each raster against the accuracy bar</h2>
    <p>Each raster is rendered at its own size and scored against an unquantised render of your source, placed
      exactly like it. It passes when at most 1% of pixels are off by more than 8/255
      in any channel, and the edge-weighted luma error is at most 1.5883, on both a black and a white background.
      The second clause catches serrated edges that the first lets through.</p>
    <p>The candidates are quality 40, 50, 60, 70, 80, 90, 95, 98 and 99 at the full palette, and quality 100 at 2 to
      256 colours: 26 encodes. Each one is already the smaller of a quantised and a lossless PNG. The effort decides
      how many are tried:</p>
    <div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Effort</th><th>Encodes</th><th>Zopfli</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {efforts.map(([name, encodes, zopfli]) => (<tr><td><code>{name}</code></td><td>{encodes}</td><td>{zopfli}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>If quality 80 fails and another candidate passes, that candidate wins even when it is larger: the bar is a
      constraint, not one side of a trade. If nothing passes, as on the 48 px ICO of a very dense grid, the raster
      keeps the quality-80 encode. A pinned <code>--quality</code> or <code>--colors</code> makes one encode at
      that setting and warns when it misses the bar. The Vite and Astro dev servers always use <code>fast</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Finishing and packing</h2>
    <p>Zopfli re-deflates each chosen PNG once, after the choice, so it never runs on a losing candidate. It is
      lossless: the decoded pixels are unchanged. The order matters, because the quantiser re-encodes from scratch
      and would throw away any recompression done before it. Measured at 192 px, the wrong order is 21.0% bigger.</p>
    <p><code>favicon.ico</code> is a 22-byte header followed by the 48×48 PNG, unchanged. favseed writes that header
      itself, byte-identical to <code>icotool -c -r</code>; the BMP payload icotool writes by default is 8.8 times
      larger on the fixtures. The optional manifest lists only the padded PNGs, and the build prints
      the <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> tags for your <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. <a href="/outputs">Outputs</a> describes
      every file and the markup it needs.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where it runs</h2>
    <p>The command line needs Node 24 or newer. Its image engine, <code>@napi-rs/image</code>, carries resvg, oxipng
      and a quantiser in one native module, prebuilt for Linux, macOS and Windows on x64 and arm64, and FreeBSD on
      x64. Zopfli is a WebAssembly build of Google's source. The Vite plugin and the Astro integration run the same
      build and cache its result, keyed on favseed's version, the source, the options and the engine versions.</p>
    <p>This site runs the same pipeline in a worker, on the WebAssembly build of the same engine. It needs HTTPS or
      localhost, WebAssembly and <code>DecompressionStream</code>. Where the browser allows shared memory it uses
      the threaded engine, and otherwise a single-thread build that is slower but gives the same files. The engine
      is about 10.3 MB before transfer compression and loads only once you point at, focus or drag onto the file picker. Your file never leaves the
      tab.</p>
    <p>Matching self-contained SVGs and options give identical bytes everywhere. CI builds the test fixtures on
      Linux, macOS and Windows and compares the hashes, and runs this site in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit against
      the native output. Outline text and embed images as <code>data:</code> URLs, because fonts and external files
      can differ between machines. Every number above comes from a measurement;
      the <a href="/benchmarks">benchmarks</a> collect the results,
      and <a href="https://github.com/cevdetch/favseed/blob/main/docs/DECISIONS.md">the decisions</a> record why each
      step is the way it is.</p>
  </section>
</Doc>
